<bookinfo>
<title>The kbluetooth Handbook</title>

<authorgroup>
<author><othername>The KDE3 revival developers</othername></author>
<!-- TRANS:ROLES_OF_TRANSLATORS -->
</authorgroup>

<legalnotice>&FDLNotice;</legalnotice>

<date>2026-11-23</date>
<releaseinfo>0.1</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>kbluetooth shows the Bluetooth adapter and devices in the system
tray. It switches Bluetooth on and off, connects and disconnects paired
devices, and finds and pairs new ones. It talks to BlueZ 5
(<command>bluetoothd</command>) over the system D-Bus.</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>Bluetooth</keyword>
<keyword>BlueZ</keyword>
<keyword>kbluetooth</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Using kbluetooth</title>

<para>kbluetooth starts with the session and puts an icon in the system
tray. The icon is grey when Bluetooth is off, blocked or missing, blue
when it is on, and blue with a green dot when a device is connected. The
tooltip names the connected devices and their battery charge, when the
device reports it.</para>

<sect1 id="popup">
<title>The popup</title>
<para>A click with the left mouse button opens the popup: the
<guilabel>Bluetooth on</guilabel> switch of the adapter, the paired
devices and <guibutton>Scan for Devices...</guibutton>. A click on a device
connects it, or disconnects it when it is connected; a click with the right
mouse button opens the menu of the device. The tooltip of a device shows
its address and the profiles (services) it offers.</para>
<para>When Bluetooth cannot be used the popup says why: no adapter, the
Bluetooth service <command>bluetoothd</command> is not running, or the
radio is blocked (rfkill). A block by a hardware switch or key is lifted
with that switch or key. A software block needs administrator rights,
for example <userinput><command>rfkill unblock bluetooth</command></userinput>;
kbluetooth does not lift it itself.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="pairing">
<title>Pairing a device</title>
<para><guibutton>Scan for Devices...</guibutton> opens a window that looks
for devices while it is open. Switch the device to pairing mode, select it
and press <guibutton>Pair</guibutton>. Depending on the device kbluetooth
asks to compare a passkey, to type a PIN code, or shows a PIN or passkey to
type on the device. A paired device is trusted and connected.</para>
<para>kbluetooth is the pairing agent of BlueZ: it also asks when a
device pairs from its side, or when a device that is not trusted wants to
use a service.</para>
<para>Audio devices need nothing more: when a headset or speaker is
connected, BlueZ and the sound server (PipeWire or PulseAudio) offer it as
a sound device.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="menu">
<title>The menu</title>
<para>The menu of the right mouse button has the power switch, a submenu
for each paired device (<guimenuitem>Connect</guimenuitem> or
<guimenuitem>Disconnect</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Rename...</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Trusted</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Remove...</guimenuitem>,
which deletes the pairing), the adapter to use when there are several, and
the settings:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guimenuitem>Show the Icon Without an Adapter</guimenuitem>:
off hides the icon while there is no adapter; it comes back with the
adapter, or when kbluetooth is started again.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenuitem>Switch Bluetooth On at Login</guimenuitem>:
switches the adapter on once when kbluetooth starts or the adapter
appears.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guimenuitem>Start kbluetooth at Login</guimenuitem>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>The notifications (device connected, disconnected, errors) are set
in the Control Center, under Notifications.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="testing">
<title>Testing</title>
<para>kbluetooth uses the system bus that <envar>DBUS_SYSTEM_BUS_ADDRESS</envar>
names, when it is set, and reads the rfkill state from the directory
<envar>KBLUETOOTH_RFKILL_DIR</envar> instead of
<filename>/sys/class/rfkill</filename>. Both are meant for tests against a
mock BlueZ on a private bus.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="credits">
<title>Credits and License</title>
<para>kbluetooth was written for the KDE3 revival.</para>
&underFDL;
&underGPL;
</sect1>

</chapter>

